## 1. MATHEMATICAL FOUNDATIONS

### 1.1 Spectral Signal Model (NIR and Raman)

The raw optical signal across wavelength channels λ₁, ..., λₙ is modeled as a linear mixture of pure-component spectra with additive Gaussian noise:

**x(λ) = Σᵢ cᵢ · εᵢ(λ) + η(λ), η(λ) ~ N(0, σ²)**

For NIR absorbance, the Beer-Lambert law gives:

**A(λ) = log₁₀(I₀/I) = Σᵢ εᵢ(λ) · cᵢ · l**

where I₀ and I are incident and transmitted intensities, l is the optical path length (cm), and εᵢ(λ) is the molar absorptivity (L·mol⁻¹·cm⁻¹) of component i. NIR spectroscopy utilizes the absorption of near-infrared light by hydrogen-containing groups within a sample, enabling extraction of specific information regarding the characteristics of these groups within organic molecules. The application of chemometrics in NIR spectral analysis reduces the dataset size, excludes noise interference, and improves the efficiency of multivariate correction, while finite variable model construction reduces random errors and improves prediction accuracy. [Springer](https://link.springer.com/article/10.1186/s43088-025-00660-8)

---

### 1.2 Spectral Pre-processing Pipeline

Four sequential transformations are applied to every raw spectrum before any chemometric modelling.

**Step 1 — Savitzky-Golay Smoothing (1st/2nd derivative):**

**x̂(λⱼ) = Σₖ₌₋ₘᵐ hₖ · x(λⱼ₊ₖ)**

where hₖ are polynomial least-squares convolution coefficients of degree p within a window of width 2m+1. The derivative form (p=2 or p=3) simultaneously smooths and removes polynomial baseline drift. Peak detection in DPV voltammograms uses the zero-crossing of the Savitzky-Golay 2nd derivative.

**Step 2 — Standard Normal Variate (SNV):**

**x_SNV(λ) = [x(λ) − x̄] / sₓ**

Removes multiplicative scattering effects by centering and scaling each spectrum to zero mean, unit variance.

**Step 3 — Multiplicative Scatter Correction (MSC):**

**x_MSC(λ) = [x(λ) − b] / a, where (a, b) = argmin ‖x − (a·m + b)‖²**

m(λ) is the reference mean spectrum of the calibration set. OLS regression estimates a and b per sample.

**Step 4 — L2 Normalization:**

**x_norm = x / ‖x‖₂**

---

### 1.3 Dimensionality Reduction: Principal Component Analysis

For a data matrix X ∈ ℝⁿˣᵖ (n samples, p wavelengths), PCA decomposes via eigenanalysis of XᵀX:

**X = T · Pᵀ + E**

where T ∈ ℝⁿˣᵏ is the scores matrix (coordinates in PC space), P ∈ ℝᵖˣᵏ contains the loading vectors (eigenvectors), and E is the residual noise matrix. PCA provides a transformation of the original data into a new coordinate system of principal components, where each component represents a trend within the original data set. This reduces the dimensionality of the data whilst retaining as much of the variation as possible. PCA is computationally light, has low noise sensitivity, does not require user input, and can be used to group spectra based on their results in a scores plot. [uspto](https://image-ppubs.uspto.gov/dirsearch-public/print/downloadPdf/11435292) Typically k = 5–20 components explain >95% of spectral variance.

---

### 1.4 Quantitative Calibration: Partial Least Squares Regression

PLSR simultaneously decomposes X and the analyte concentration matrix Y to find latent variables (LVs) that maximally predict Y:

**X = T·Pᵀ + Eₓ**
**Y = U·Qᵀ + Eᵧ**
**Ŷ = X · W*(PᵀW*)⁻¹Qᵀ**

where T and U are X- and Y-scores, P and Q are loadings, and W* are modified weights. The number of LVs A is optimized by k-fold cross-validation minimizing:

**RMSE = √[ (1/n) Σᵢ (yᵢ − ŷᵢ)² ]**

Key validation metrics: RMSECV (cross-validation error), RMSEP (test set prediction error), R² (coefficient of determination), and RPD = σᵧ / RMSEP. An RPD > 3 is considered excellent; RPD > 5 is outstanding. The NIR and SERS spectral feature-layer fusion method (HSIC-VSIO + PLSR) demonstrates significant superiority over single spectral techniques in detecting pesticide residues in complex matrix samples, achieving RMSE_cal = 0.160, RMSE_pred = 0.185, R² = 0.988, and RPD = 8.290. [nih](https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC11977969/)

