### 1.5 Classification: PLS-DA and Deep Learning Benchmarks

For categorical outputs (contaminated/clean, adulterated/authentic, GMO-positive/negative), PLS-DA encodes class membership as a dummy Y-matrix and assigns:

**class(i) = argmax_c [Xᵢ · β]_c**

Performance is evaluated via the Matthews Correlation Coefficient (MCC), preferred over accuracy for imbalanced food safety datasets:

**MCC = (TP·TN − FP·FN) / √[(TP+FP)(TP+FN)(TN+FP)(TN+FN)]**

Additional metrics: Sensitivity = TP/(TP+FN), Specificity = TN/(TN+FP), AUC-ROC for probabilistic classifiers.

1D and 2D CNN models are developing well in residue detection, requiring no manually pretreated data and having good ability to learn representative spectral features from the original signal. One-dimensional residual networks (1D-ResNet) and spectral convolutional models have shown better performance than traditional chemometric techniques such as PLS-DA and SVM in classifying contaminated samples, effectively capturing subtle spectral changes caused by pesticide molecules with improved detection accuracy. Transformer-based architectures such as SpecTransformer can capture long-range relationships and complex feature interactions within spectral data through self-attention mechanisms. [PubMed Central](https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12692005/)

Validated benchmarks (2024–2025 literature):

| Algorithm | F1 Score Range | Key Strength | Application |
|---|---|---|---|
| PLS-DA | 0.87–0.93 | Interpretable spectral loadings | Adulteration screening |
| SVM (RBF kernel) | 0.90–0.95 | Strong small-sample performance | Pesticide classification |
| Random Forest | 0.91–0.96 | Robust to outliers | Multi-contaminant screening |
| 1D-ResNet | 0.94–0.99 | Automated feature learning | Spectral classification |
| SpecTransformer | 0.95–0.99 | Long-range spectral context | Complex matrix analysis |
| CNN-PLSR Hybrid | 0.96–0.99 | Quantification + classification | Full pipeline |

