### 1.7 Electrochemical Heavy Metal Quantification (DPV)

For Differential Pulse Voltammetry, the peak current is governed by the Randles-Ševčík-type relation adapted for pulsed techniques:

**Iₚ = n·F·A·D^(1/2)·c · (nFΔE/4RT) · [exp(nFΔE/2RT) − 1] / [exp(nFΔE/2RT) + 1]**

where n = electrons transferred, F = Faraday constant (96,485 C/mol), A = electrode area (cm²), D = diffusion coefficient (cm²/s), c = analyte concentration (mol/cm³), ΔE = pulse amplitude (V), R = gas constant (8.314 J/mol·K), T = temperature (K).

The Limit of Detection is: **LOD = 3σ_blank / S**, where S is the slope of the calibration curve (nA/µg·L⁻¹).

Portable electrochemical sensors, especially when combined with lab-on-chip technologies, show high sensitivity, selectivity, and ease of use. One sensor achieved LODs of 0.5698 µg/L for Cd²⁺, 0.4024 µg/L for Pb²⁺, and 0.2565 µg/L for Hg²⁺, with high reliability demonstrated by RSD below 7% and recovery rates ranging from 96.4% to 106.2%. [PubMed Central](https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12467198/)

