### 1.8 Electronic Tongue Sensor Array: Nikolsky-Eisenman Model

The Electronic Tongue comprises m cross-selective electrodes (ion-selective, redox-active, and bio-functionalized). For each potentiometric electrode j, the measured potential in a food extract is governed by the Nikolsky-Eisenman equation:

**Eⱼ = E°ⱼ + (RT/zⱼF) · ln[ aⱼ + Σₖ≠ⱼ K^pot_jk · aₖ^(zⱼ/zₖ) ] + εⱼ**

where aⱼ are ion activities, K^pot_jk are potentiometric selectivity coefficients for interfering ion k on electrode j, zⱼ are ionic charges, and εⱼ is measurement noise. The full response vector E = (E₁, ..., Eₘ) forms the "electrochemical taste fingerprint." For voltammetric e-tongue variants, pulsed waveforms replace open-circuit potential measurements, increasing information content per electrode.

Electronic tongue technologies provide rapid, objective, and reliable alternatives for food quality monitoring, mimicking human gustatory functions through sensor arrays and advanced data processing techniques including artificial intelligence and pattern recognition algorithms. [MDPI](https://www.mdpi.com/2227-9040/13/5/161) A recently developed electronic tongue is capable of identifying differences in similar liquids such as milk with varying water content, diverse products including soda types and coffee blends, signs of spoilage in fruit juices, and instances of food safety concerns. Results were even more accurate when artificial intelligence used its own assessment parameters to interpret the data generated by the electronic tongue. [ScienceDaily](https://www.sciencedaily.com/releases/2024/10/241009121229.htm)

The extracted 16-dimensional ET feature vector feeds PLS, ANN, or Random Forest models for freshness classification, biogenic amine index prediction, organic acid profiling, and adulteration scoring.

