### 1.9 CRISPR-Cas12a Assay: Kinetics and Signal Model

The integrated microfluidic CRISPR-Cas12a assay proceeds in three mathematically describable stages.

**Stage 1 — Isothermal Amplification (RPA/LAMP):**
Exponential amplification of target DNA from initial copy number N₀:

**[DNA](t) = N₀ · exp(t / τₐ)**

where τₐ is the amplification time constant (typically τₐ ≈ 2–5 min for RPA at 37–42°C).

**Stage 2 — CRISPR Trans-Cleavage (Michaelis-Menten kinetics):**
Active Cas12a-crRNA complexes cleave fluorophore-quencher reporter probes. When activated by target dsDNA, collateral cleavage proceeds as:

**d[F]/dt = Vmax · [DNA] / (Kₘ + [DNA])**

where [F] is cleaved fluorophore concentration, Vmax = kcat · [Cas12a]_total, and Kₘ is the Michaelis constant.

**Stage 3 — Fluorescence Signal:**

**S(t) = φ · [F](t)**

where φ = quantum yield × collection efficiency of the CMOS detector.

**Limit of Detection:** The minimum target copy number at which S(t_end) > 3σ_blank. Validated in literature:

Combining RPA technology with Cas12a trans-cleavage reaction enables detection of P-CaMV 35S at 10 copies/µL of standard plasmid and T-NOS at 1 copy/µL. The entire detection process can be completed at 37°C within 40 minutes. [ScienceDirect](https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S095671352200576X)

A deep learning-assisted, smartphone-based multi-RPA-CRISPR/Cas12a-G4 colorimetric platform achieves a 1 aM LOD for CaMV35S and NOS in 55 minutes, with YOLOv5 deep learning algorithm trained for colorimetric detection that is more sensitive than gray value analysis alone. [ScienceDirect](https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S0956713524006649)

The CRISPR assay outputs a scalar probability γ ∈ [0,1] via logistic calibration of end-point fluorescence against a standard curve. This γ feeds directly into the multi-modal fusion layer as a definitive DNA-level evidence signal.

