### 1.10 Blockchain Data Integrity: Cryptographic Proof Layer

Each device scan generates a tamper-evident record R = (deviceID, timestamp, modality fingerprints, result vector, GPS coordinates). The device signs R with its ECDSA private key (curve secp256r1):

**σ = Sign_sk(H(R))**

where H is SHA-256. The cloud node verifies σ with the device's registered public key and aggregates records into a Merkle tree. The Merkle root is anchored on a permissioned ledger (Hyperledger Fabric) with proof-of-authority consensus every 10 minutes.

Zero-knowledge proofs are a powerful cryptographic tool that can validate the authenticity of goods, certifications, and transaction records in supply chain management systems without exposing sensitive business information. By utilizing ZKPs, participants can maintain data integrity and prove provenance while keeping proprietary details confidential, and supply chain transparency is maintained for authorized parties only. [Springer](https://link.springer.com/chapter/10.1007/978-981-97-0088-2_3)

zk-SNARKs are applied to prove that a scan result lies within regulatory limits without revealing the raw spectrum — enabling public compliance audit while protecting proprietary spectral data and business-sensitive supply chain information.

Blockchain technology has significantly improved food safety through empirical and theoretical work on supply chain traceability, transparency, fraud prevention, and compliance management. IBM Food Trust reduced traceability time for food products from several days to seconds, greatly enhancing consumer trust and operational efficiency. [ScienceDirect](https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S0924224425006259)

