### 2.2 NIR Spectroscopy Subsystem

Compact NIR and Raman devices supported by embedded AI algorithms are increasingly capable of delivering laboratory-grade performance in the field, in clinical settings, and in industrial environments. A major emerging trend is the deep integration of AI and machine learning into every stage of the spectroscopic pipeline, with AI-powered models now outperforming traditional chemometric approaches in tasks such as spectral deconvolution, feature extraction, calibration transfer, and uncertainty quantification. [MDPI](https://www.mdpi.com/1424-8220/26/3/989)

Hardware specifications:
- Light source: Broadband tungsten-halogen or NIR LED array (800–2500 nm)
- Detector: InGaAs photodiode array (900–1700 nm core; extended InGaAs to 2500 nm optional)
- Spectral resolution: ≤ 10 nm FWHM
- Scan time: 2–10 seconds; signal averaging over 16–64 co-adds
- Sampling geometry: Diffuse reflectance (solids), transmittance (liquids and semi-solids)
- Reference database: Global food spectral library, >50,000 reference spectra

Key validated applications: dairy (protein, fat, moisture), meat and fish (freshness, species), fruits and vegetables (sugar, pesticides), cereals (protein, starch), honey adulteration, beverage authentication.

