### 2.3 SERS Subsystem

Raman spectroscopy offers high sensitivity, fast analysis, no need for sample pretreatment, and unique fingerprint structure information. It has found extensive application in food safety monitoring including identification of foodborne pathogens, pesticides, heavy metals, and mycotoxins. [Wiley Online Library](https://iadns.onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/10.1002/fsh3.12040)

Water, the main component of food samples, is a weak Raman scatterer and never interferes with Raman signals of other constituents. NIR Raman spectroscopy achieves relatively deep penetration into meat tissues with reduced autofluorescence interference. Raman spectrum intensity is concentration-dependent, making quantitative analysis possible, and Raman spectroscopy can recognize multiple molecules simultaneously with fingerprint identification, enabling rapid online monitoring or field detection. [ScienceDirect](https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S2214799322001126)

Hardware specifications:
- Laser excitation: 785 nm (NIR Raman — minimizes food matrix autofluorescence)
- Power: < 50 mW at sample surface
- Detector: Cooled CCD or CMOS array, quantum efficiency > 90% at 785 nm
- Spectral resolution: ≤ 4 cm⁻¹ over 200–3200 cm⁻¹
- SERS substrate: Disposable gold nanoparticle (40–80 nm)-coated microfluidic chip
- Surface functionalization: Aptamers or Molecularly Imprinted Polymers (MIPs) for selective analyte capture
- LOD for organophosphate pesticides: 0.1–10 µg/kg
- Scan time: 5–30 seconds per acquisition

