## 7. ORIGINAL PhD RESEARCH CONTRIBUTIONS — OPEN PROBLEMS

**1. Unified Five-Modality Calibration Transfer Framework.** Extending Piecewise Direct Standardization (PDS) to simultaneously harmonize NIR, Raman, ET, DPV, and CRISPR fluorescence outputs across a population of heterogeneous devices. The mathematical challenge is that each modality has a different dimensionality (p₁ >> p₂ >> p₃ >> 1) and a different noise model, requiring a modality-aware block-diagonal transfer matrix with separate regularization per modality.

**2. Uncertainty-Aware End-to-End Bayesian Multi-Modal Fusion.** Designing a Transformer-based deep fusion architecture where measurement uncertainty (from photon shot noise, electrode drift, CRISPR fluorescence variance) is propagated end-to-end through all modalities and the attention mechanism, producing statistically calibrated confidence intervals on every predicted concentration and classification probability. This requires combining Bayesian deep learning with multi-modal fusion in a way not yet demonstrated in the food safety literature.

**3. Physics-Informed Neural Network for CRISPR Kinetics.** Embedding the Michaelis-Menten kinetic model and the RPA amplification ODE directly into a physics-informed neural network (PINN) that fits CRISPR fluorescence time-series in real time, enabling continuous (not end-point) LOD estimation and real-time detection of inhibited reactions. This has been demonstrated in biochemical systems but not in portable food safety CRISPR cartridges.

**4. Spectral and Electrochemical Explainability for Regulatory Science.** Adapting SHAP values, attention rollout, and integrated gradients to 1D spectral CNN and ET models to produce molecular-level justifications (e.g., "classification driven by Raman peak at 1045 cm⁻¹ — consistent with chlorpyrifos P=O stretch") for every safety alert. This is a prerequisite for regulatory acceptance of AI-based food safety decisions in jurisdictions governed by EU AI Act and FDA guidelines.

**5. Few-Shot and Meta-Learning for Novel Contaminant Detection.** Designing MAML-based (Model-Agnostic Meta-Learning) or prototypical network frameworks that allow the FIS cloud model to detect a previously unseen pesticide or mycotoxin from as few as 5–10 reference spectra. The spectral few-shot problem has specific structure (spectral smoothness, known molecular group absorptions) that could be exploited through physics-constrained meta-learning priors.

**6. Microfluidic Cartridge Multiphysics Optimisation.** Combining computational fluid dynamics (Navier-Stokes), heat transfer (Fourier conduction), and reaction kinetics (RPA + CRISPR ODEs) in a finite-element simulation of the entire CRISPR cartridge to analytically optimize channel geometry, Peltier placement, reagent chamber volumes, and mixing efficiency — producing a validated design tool for low-cost mass production scale-up.

