Appendix C: AI and Machine Learning Architecture

C.1 Two-Tier Intelligence Model

The FIS employs a two-tier AI architecture, balancing on-device speed with cloud-based accuracy.

Tier 1 (Edge AI):

· Processor: ARM Cortex-A55 CPU with a dedicated 2 TOPS Neural Processing Unit (NPU). The NXP i.MX 95 processor combines Cortex-A55 cores, a Cortex-M7/M33 controller, a Mali GPU, and an NPU to deliver up to 2 TOPS for high-resolution data processing and real-time decision-making.
· Role: Real-time pre-processing and binary pass/fail screening.
· Model: A quantized 8-bit integer 1D-CNN model.
· Tasks: Savitzky-Golay filtering, SNV normalization, DPV peak detection (via 2nd-derivative zero-crossing), and end-point CRISPR fluorescence readout.

Tier 2 (Cloud AI):

· Role: Full multi-modal fusion and comprehensive analysis.
· Model: A deep learning architecture composed of parallel feature extractors for each modality, a cross-modal Transformer attention fusion layer, and multi-task prediction heads.
· Uncertainty Quantification: Monte Carlo Dropout is used at inference time to provide predictive mean and variance.

C.2 Deep Learning Models for Spectral Data

Recent research shows that deep learning models, particularly 1D-CNNs and 1D-ResNets, can outperform traditional chemometric methods like PLS-DA and SVM by automatically extracting robust features from raw spectral data without manual pre-processing. The 1D-SE-ResNet, which enhances a 1D-ResNet with squeeze-and-excitation blocks, has proven very effective for near-infrared spectroscopy applications, such as assessing pork freshness.

